{"id": 58, "ts": 1784098552.3453002, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 3, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I acknowledge the valid point raised by Agent\u202f3 about the missing concrete examples of Candidate\u202fB\u2019s safety\u2011critical decision\u2011making. However, the evidence we do have still positions B as the stronger overall fit. B is described as **very conscientious** and **handles stress very well**, both core to operational reliability and stress resilience. His ability to **assess weather conditions** and his **excellent computer skills** demonstrate solid technical and cognitive competence essential for long\u2011distance flights. While B does have concerns\u2014making nasty remarks to colleagues and a relatively weak memory for numbers\u2014these issues do not directly undermine his capacity to make correct, timely decisions under pressure, especially given his proven stress handling.\nIn contrast, Candidate\u202fC, while impressive in hazard anticipation and leadership, still lacks any documented evidence of **crew cooperation** or **professional communication** (I asked Agent\u202f3 and received no observations). The noted concerns about C being a \u201cshow\u2011off,\u201d not open to new ideas, and unfriendly further raise doubts about his ability to maintain a collaborative cockpit environment. Without clear proof that C can work constructively with crew, the risk to crew cooperation outweighs his hazard\u2011anticipation strength.\nTherefore, I maintain my provisional vote for **Candidate\u202fB**, pending any future evidence that directly demonstrates B\u2019s decision\u2011quality in safety\u2011critical scenarios or that clarifies C\u2019s teamwork behavior."}
